 first, baby. The head just takes minutes.' Kate Sullivan stopped running. She turned around and went to find him."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Choice', 'text': 'a handwritten letter got a place of honor on the mantel — not as a relic, but as a reminder: that love lost can be love found, that the heart knows first, and that home was never a town at all.\n\nHe laughed through tears, the same laugh, and the sunset did its thing, and Rosewood held its breath again. This time nobody interrupted. This time they stayed.\n\nher neighbor Mrs. Abernathy cried at the news and denied it. The whole of Rosewood seemed to conspire in their happiness: the bookshop stayed open late, the bakery made a cake, the wildflowers bloomed on schedule.'}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'The Promise', 'text': "They danced on the porch to the mixtape — no, to a new playlist, because the past is a place to visit, not to live. Kate Sullivan had learned that at last.\n\nLate that night, on the shore, they stood where the boy and girl had stood. 'We made it,' he said. 'We were always going to,' she said, and believed it, the heart knowing first.\n\nHe proposed with a handwritten letter — fitting, her neighbor Mrs. Abernathy said, since it started everything. Kate Sullivan said yes before he finished asking, because seventeen years is long enough to wait for a sentence."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Epilogue in Wildflowers', 'text': "Kate Sullivan walks the shore most evenings, sometimes alone, sometimes with him, always with Rosewood around her like a held breath. The sunsets haven't changed. She has — into someone who stays.\n\nher neighbor Mrs. Abernathy still diagnoses everything with pie. Her grandmother Rose's quilt is worn thin with love. her overbearing mother is a story they tell laughing, a ghost with no haunting left in it.\n\nAnd when the children ask about a handwritten letter, Kate Sullivan tells them: it was a map. Not to a place — to a person. To the bravest thing she ever did, which was to come home."}]}

