e: run, before it breaks."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Choice', 'text': "He laughed through tears, the same laugh, and the sunset did its thing, and Willow Creek held its breath again. This time nobody interrupted. This time they stayed.\n\na recipe box got a place of honor on the mantel — not as a relic, but as a reminder: that love lost can be love found, that the heart knows first, and that home was never a town at all.\n\nShe found him at the shore, where it started. 'I choose,' she said, breathless, 'the thing that scares me. I choose you. I've always chosen you — I just took seventeen years to say it.'"}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'The Promise', 'text': "Late that night, on the shore, they stood where the boy and girl had stood. 'We made it,' he said. 'We were always going to,' she said, and believed it, the heart knowing first.\n\nthe heart knows first — Sophie Lane wrote it in the new journal, the one for the next seventeen years. In Willow Creek, under the stars, with love: the whole story, and only just beginning.\n\nThe wedding was in Willow Creek, in the season of wildflowers. Her grandmother Rose's quilt was on the bed. her sister Lily gave a speech that made everyone cry laughing."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Epilogue in Wildflowers', 'text': "the heart knows first. In Willow Creek, in the wildflowers, in the salt air, in every sunset: the whole story, still beginning, never ending.\n\nher sister Lily still diagnoses everything with pie. Her grandmother Rose's quilt is worn thin with love. old heartbreak is a story they tell laughing, a ghost with no haunting left in it.\n\nAnd when the children ask about a recipe box, Sophie Lane tells them: it was a map. Not to a place — to a person. To the bravest thing she ever did, which was to come home."}]}

def word_count():
    return sum(len(c["text"].split()) for c in BOOK["chapters"])

def print_book(ch=None):
    print("=" * 64)
    print(BOOK["title"].upper(), "\nby", BOOK["author"], "-", BOOK["genre"])
    print("=" * 64)
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        if ch and c["n"] != ch:
            continue
        print("\n\nCHAPTER %d: %s\n%s" % (c["n"], c["title"], "-" * 40))
        print(c["text"])
    print("\n[%d words, %d chapters — complete]" % (word_count(), len(BOOK["chapters"])))

def search(word):
    w = word.lower()
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        for i, para in enumerate(c["text"].split("\n\n")):
            if w in para.lower():
                print("[ch %d, para %d] %s..." % (c["n"], i + 1, para[:160].replace("\n", " ")))

def write_html(path="book.html"):
    parts = ["<html><head><meta charset='utf-8'><title>%s</title></head><body>" % _h.escape(BOOK["title"])]
    parts.append("<h1>%s</h1><p><i>by %s — %s</i></p>" % (_h.escape(BOOK["title"]), _h.escape(BOOK["author"]), _h.escape(BOOK["genre"])))
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        parts.append("<h2>Chapter %d: %s</h2>" % (c["n"], _h.escape(c["title"])))
        for para in c["text"].split("\n\n"):
            parts.append("<p>%s</p>" % _h.escape(para))
    parts.append("</body></html>")
    open(path, "w").write("\n".join(parts))
    print("wrote", path)

if __name__ == "__main__":
    a = sys.argv[1:]
    if a and a[0] == "html":
        write_html()
    elif len(a) >= 2 and a[0] == "search":
        search(a[1])
    elif a and a[0].isdigit():
        print_book(int(a[0]))
    else:
        print_book()

